          "term": "somalia documents",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "oxygen plants",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "covid pandemic",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "covid vaccines",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "medical oxygen",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "covid response",
          "heading": false
        }
      ],
      "extract": "This is the third report in the series documenting the work of the World Health Organization (WHO) in managing and responding to the coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) pandemic in Somalia. The first of these reports was published in 2020 to mark six months from the start of the pandemic in Somalia, while the second report was published in 2021 to mark one year of the COVID-19 pandemic in the country1. This report",
      "extractCut": true
    },
    {
      "id": "sec-001-promoting-science-evidence-and-knowledge-transla",
      "number": null,
      "title": "Promoting science, evidence and knowledge: translating research into actions on health",
      "level": 2,
      "pageStart": 32,
      "pageEnd": 42,
      "labelStart": "25",
      "labelEnd": "xlii",
      "words": 1610,
      "summary": {
        "text": "Describes how WHO Somalia used data, research and digital tools such as EWARN, dashboards, a community health worker app, chatbots and CommCare vaccine registration covering over 1.3 million people to guide its COVID-19 response. It reports on research studies, publications and information notes produced, which built local research capacity. The final section draws lessons for an inclusive recovery, arguing for investment in primary health care, essential public health functions and the health workforce to build resilience and progress towards universal health coverage, followed by acknowledgements of donors.",
        "status": "draft"
      },
